Julija Budny: Thank you, Jorge. If I could comment on this, I think that today's global digital cooperation has reached an inflection point. The distribution of the benefits of connectivity is very unbalanced. The phenomenon of exclusion in the digital world manifests itself in different forms, including a gender gap in digital exclusion, which prevents a large number of people from reaping the benefits of the digital revolution. Social and economic injustices threaten the development and progress of democracy. Bold commitments must be made to ensure that Agenda 2030 can be successfully realized. It is clear that a Global Digital Compact can make a meaningful contribution to these ambitious goals. In doing so, we need to:

elaborate benefits of digitalization to all countries and all people;

invest in transparent, inclusive and accountable approaches to governing digital resources so as to safeguard the public Internet;

develop policies and laws that encourage meaningful innovation. We must adopt an uncompromising, paradigm-changing approach to tackle the contradiction between digital inequality and digital inclusion. While connectivity is expanding, digital inequality is increasing because all technologies are currently steered towards a phenomenon far more fundamental than digital technologies. It is about the digitalization itself. The future of the digital revolution in the Global South is in serious doubt. Suffice it to say that this is not simply about access to the Internet, but also about guaranteed access to high-quality, affordable connectivity and equal participation in all those trading regimes setting the rules under which the countries and peoples of the South will have to struggle. All people need can be used to take advantage of the opportunities enabled by this new paradigm. Basically, we need to bridge the gap between those who, thanks to their technological resources and financial means, are able to make use of the Internet and other forms of digital technology for economic transactions to gain prosperity and generate national wealth and those who can't. The great powers are using free trade agreements to undermine the digital rights of people and countries in the South. Trade rules are also used to force governments to open up data flows to a degree that leaves local public authorities no freedom of action in governing transnational entities. Monopoly firms and their algorithms prevent external control of source code, while at the same time allowing developing countries to become more systematically dependent on data and artificial intelligence power holders just like economic dependence. This is infrastructural dependence that takes people back to a neo-colonial, extractivist system. It's important not to forget the unfinished business left by the WSIS. It's worth looking back at WSIS to clarify the need for a Global Digital Compact as well as for the exchange of technologies that emerged in the past couple of decades. It is also necessary to facilitate the political, regulatory, technical and financial conditions for increasing the agency and choice of individuals and collective actors; the autonomy to connect and position themselves in the digital space in a way that reinforces the democratic conditions where people can actually vote and participate in democratic processes. It's important that WSIS can in some way become a basis for human rights, and for an intersectional, feminist approach to deal with the geopolitics of global inequality and injustice. I am in agreement with Macrae when he emphasizes that the public, collective and social value of digitalization as well as the freedoms it fosters will finally determine the quality of the future transition that can lead us to a well-guided digitalization process.
